<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mestizo">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mestizo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mestizo, Spanish for mixed person, is an ethno-cultural category used mainly in the Hispanic world for people whose identity derives mostly from mixing between Spanish and Native heritage. It began as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casta">castas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s colonies, a formal label in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>, parish registers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> trials; mestizos were the first group set apart from Spaniards and enslaved Africans, and stood above other castas, since they paid no tribute and their men could be ordained priests. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> and independence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system disappeared, legal racial categories ending in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1824, and in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> the word has become largely a cultural term.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1837 Rafael Carrera, a young <b>mestizo</b> swineherd, led an insurgency of peasants and indigenous villagers in eastern Guatemala, using guerrilla tactics against government troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Society remained shaped by colonial hierarchy, with a creole elite of landowners and merchants above <b>mestizo</b> and pardo artisans and farmers, indigenous communities and enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…José Vasconcelos, minister of education from 1921, sponsored the murals of Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros, which influenced public art in the United States and Latin America, and promoted a celebration of the country’s Indigenous and <b>mestizo</b> heritage that became central to national identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was baptised as Diego de Castro around 1568 and relied on a <b>mestizo</b> secretary, Martín de Pando, for his dealings in Spanish.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Today Ecuador has about 18 million people, mostly <b>mestizo</b>, with large indigenous, Afro-Ecuadorian and montubio minorities, and remittances from emigrants in Spain and the United States are an important source of income.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It had about 6 million people at the 2024 census, overwhelmingly <b>mestizo</b> and Catholic, with a large and growing evangelical minority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
